Title: Does an uninstall withdraw the sitter indexing consent
Created: 2026-09-12
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-12T09:02Z claude created

2026-09-12T09:07Z claude note filed from the lifecycle audit (F1). Needs a ruling: 'asked once per profile' is ratified and conflicts with 'an uninstall withdraws consent'
2026-09-12T10:37Z MinhHaDuong ruled — an uninstall writes the indexing switch off rather than clearing it: consent withdrawn, and the first-run question not re-asked. Neither option as put; recorded in DECISIONS.md and SPEC 5.2.7, implementation handed to 0773 Action 2
--- body ---

## Context

Audit finding F1 (`verification/SITTER-LIFECYCLE-AUDIT-2026-09-12.md`).

`extensions.sdt-pack-sitter.enabled` holds the answer to the first-run indexing
question. `uninstall()` is empty and nothing anywhere clears the pref, so a
profile that answered *yes*, removed the add-on, and later installs it again
starts indexing without asking. The prompt is the consent record for a
background process that reads the whole library; an uninstall is the plainest
withdrawal of that consent a user can perform, and it currently leaves the
answer standing.

## Why this needs the author

"Asked at most once per profile" is ratified behaviour (SPEC.md §5.2.7, and
bootstrap.js's own comment at the launch prompt) and it is what makes
disable/re-enable and every later restart silent — 0727 arm 4 depends on it.
"Forget on uninstall" and "ask once per profile" are both defensible and they
conflict. Changing it is a ruling in `DECISIONS.md` first and SPEC second.

## Options, ranked

1. **Clear the pref on uninstall: consent is per installation.** One extra
   prompt for the user who removes and reinstalls — who is exactly the user who
   should see it. Cost: the clause in SPEC §5.2.7 gains "and is forgotten when
   the add-on is removed".
2. **Keep it, and declare it.** SPEC states that the answer survives removal,
   so a reader can find out. Cheapest, and leaves a reinstall silent.
3. **Clear it, and say so in the prompt.** The prompt gains a sentence about
   what removing the add-on does to the answer. Rejected on its face: the
   audit's own reading of that dialog is that it is the modal least likely to
   be read, and 0717 already moved two paragraphs out of it.

## Ruled 2026-09-12: neither option — write it OFF

The author chose a third answer, and it is better than the recommendation this
ticket carried. Clearing the preference (1) re-opens a question already
answered; keeping it (2) resumes indexing silently. Writing it `false` does
what (1) wanted — the consent is withdrawn — without doing what (1) cost: a
reinstall starts in the state the plugin already treats as one it RUNS in, with
the toolbar entry and the window present, "off" in plain words, reversible at
one click. `DECISIONS.md` carries the ruling and SPEC §5.2.7 is edited to match.

## Exit criteria

- ~~A ruling in `DECISIONS.md`, SPEC §5.2.7 edited to match~~ — done
  2026-09-12, in the same change as this note.
- The write implemented: `false` on the uninstall reason, in 0773's Action 2,
  which no longer waits on this ticket. That is all that remains here.
